      "method": "v1.1.0 case-law rebuild: authorities selected per decision point, each opinion retrieved from CourtListener and read; holding and quote taken from the opinion text; independently re-verified before release. Previous keyword-matched Justia listings were removed.",
      "verified_date": "2026-09-21",
      "key_cases": [
        {
          "case_name": "In re Guardianship of James D.K.",
          "citation": "2006 WI 68, 291 Wis. 2d 333, 718 N.W.2d 38",
          "court": "Wisconsin Supreme Court",
          "year": 2006,
          "date_decided": "2006-06-13",
          "holding": "Over a parent's objection a court may appoint a nonparent guardian of a minor only on a showing of extraordinary circumstances affecting the child's health or safety, and then only if the appointment is in the child's best interests.",
          "relevance": "It is the controlling Wisconsin standard for nonparent guardianship as a permanency outcome.",
          "quote": "a court is authorized under chapter 880 to appoint a nonparent as guardian of a minor if there exist extraordinary circumstances affecting the health or safety of the minor",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1233152/in-matter-of-the-guardianship-of-james-dk/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1233152
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Stephen R. v. Ilana C.",
          "citation": "2011 WI App 13, 331 Wis. 2d 108, 794 N.W.2d 533",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of Wisconsin",
          "year": 2010,
          "date_decided": "2010-12-08",
          "holding": "Wisconsin had initial child-custody jurisdiction under the UCCJEA and the circuit court properly exercised its discretion in awarding guardianship of the child to her maternal grandmother and step-grandfather on compelling reasons.",
          "relevance": "It is a worked example of kinship guardianship as a permanency outcome, including the jurisdictional predicate.",
          "quote": "Here, we conclude that the circuit court properly exercised its discretion in awarding guardianship of Alexis to Stephen and Ilya.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8238376/stephen-r-v-ilana-c/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 8238376
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Barstad v. Frazier",
          "citation": "118 Wis. 2d 549, 348 N.W.2d 479 (1984)",
          "court": "Wisconsin Supreme Court",
          "year": 1984,
          "date_decided": "1984-05-30",
          "holding": "In a custody dispute between a parent and a third party the best-interests test does not govern; a parent is entitled to custody unless the parent is unfit or unable to care for the child or compelling reasons exist, and only then may the court award custody to a third party on best interests.",
          "relevance": "It is the foundational Wisconsin standard that James D.K. and Stephen R. apply when guardianship or third-party custody is the permanency outcome.",
          "quote": "Compelling reasons include abandonment, persistent neglect of parental responsibilities, extended disruption of parental custody, or other similar extraordinary circumstances that would drastically affect the welfare of the child.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2177290/barstad-v-frazier/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 2177290
        }
      ],
